          "annotations": [],
          "text": "{\"matched\":\"g1\",\"evidence\":\"Goldman Sachs countered, however, that the merger clause’s language — stipulating that it covered “instruments executed and delivered” — indicates that it covered more than just any potential oral agreements that may have existed, and-“obyiously ... [included] other written, agreements that deal with the 2004 issuance.”\",\"determinable\":true,\"alignment\":0.68,\"reason\":\"Both identify uncertainty created by the phrase covering “other agreements and instruments”: the proposed issue questions which writings enter the integrated whole, while g1 questions whether related writings are incorporated at all and thereby expand the agreement and forum-selection scope. G1 is broader and focuses on incorporation’s legal effect rather than solely the documents’ unidentified boundaries.\",\"words_at_issue\":\"The proposed issue turns on “the other agreements and instruments executed and delivered in connection with the issuance of the Series 2004 Auction Bonds” and the clause’s silence about which documents satisfy that description. G1 turns on that same phrase together with “entire agreement between the parties relating to the subject matter hereof,” asking whether those words incorporate related written agreements or merely exclude prior parol evidence.\"}",
